virglrenderer: decoded pictures finish beside QEMU's GPU thread

What: END_FRAME no longer waits for the Mac's media engine on vrend's thread,
which is QEMU's GPU thread (every guest context's commands wait behind it). It
hands the picture to the decoder's own serial queue and returns; the queue
gives it to VideoToolbox (asynchronous decompression, at most 4 pictures per
decoder in flight: VideoToolbox's DecodeFrame still waits on its remote
decoder's semaphore and a synchronous message, about a third of the thread's
time at 4K on the M4 Max, so not on vrend's thread). A session that breaks
there is started again on vrend's thread at the next picture; a format change
or a session's end first waits for the queue. The output callback marks the picture's job
and wakes vrend's thread (callbacks->encode_wake, QEMU's poll fd);
virgl_video_decode_poll(), run from vrend_renderer_poll(), copies the pictures
that are in into the guest's planes, oldest first. Planes a picture is still
on its way into are marked (vrend_resource.video_pending, through
callbacks->decode_pending): before a draw, a clear or a dispatch that samples,
writes or renders to such a plane, and before a blit, a copy region, a
transfer or a copy transfer of one, every picture in flight goes in first,
waiting for each (vrend_video_ready). That covers the decoding context and
every other one (a player that imports the planes into its own GL context).
The copy then pauses the render condition of the context whose GL context is
current, not the decoding one's. Fences: each vrend fence also records the
last picture sent before it and signals only once every picture up to it is
in the guest's planes (the sync thread waits for that after the GL fence,
2 s at most, then a warning; polled fences stay unretired). A buffer the
guest destroys drops the pictures still on their way into it; a decoder that
goes away first puts its last pictures in. A picture the media engine has had
for 1 s is given up (logged). OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1: END_FRAME waits for
each picture, as before. With OMACVM_VIDEO_DEBUG=1 the per-120-frames line
says how many pictures were in flight at most.
Why: in Google Chrome's hardware decoding of YouTube 4K60 (AV1, MacBook Pro M4
Max) QEMU's GPU thread was busy 94 % of the time: 55 % of it waiting in
DecodeFrame + WaitForAsynchronousFrames (about 8 ms a picture), 20 % copying.
Chrome's compositor and Hyprland waited behind every picture, and Chrome
dropped 11-14 % of the frames against 3 % decoding on the CPU (31 % against
0.1 % for 4K VP9 on a MacBook Air M2), so Chromium was held to 1080p
(OMACVM_VA_MAX).
Applies on top of virgl-videotoolbox-decode.patch,
virgl-videotoolbox-encode-async.patch (the fence hook), virgl-video-plane-edge.patch
and virgl-video-picture-size.patch.
Upstream: OmacVM addition to its own backend; the fence hook would need an
upstream API for late video results. Not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-decode-async.c (4K H.264 from VTCompressionSession):
END_FRAME returns in less than half the time VideoToolbox takes when waited for
(without the patch: 8.3 ms against 2.5 ms on the mini M4), pictures read right
after END_FRAME, six at once, another context's copy region right after
END_FRAME, another context's clear of a plane right after END_FRAME (the draw
path's check), a fence after END_FRAME then the GL texture read past vrend
(fails without the fence hook), a buffer and the decoder destroyed with
pictures in flight; polled fences, sync-thread fences and
OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1.
test-video-decode.c and test-video-padding.c (they read through transfers)
pass unchanged; test-video-decode.c also with OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1.
